6502 CPU Emulator
==================
A minimal emulator for the MOS 6502 microprocessor, written in C++.
Based on the classic tutorial approach from
https://www.masswerk.at/6502/6502_instruction_set.html

STATUS
Implements the full official 6502 instruction set (151 opcodes, no
illegal/undocumented opcodes):
  - Loads/stores: LDA, LDX, LDY, STA, STX, STY (all addressing modes)
  - Transfers: TAX, TAY, TXA, TYA, TSX, TXS
  - Stack: PHA, PHP, PLA, PLP
  - Logic: AND, EOR, ORA, BIT
  - Arithmetic: ADC, SBC, CMP, CPX, CPY
  - Inc/dec: INC, INX, INY, DEC, DEX, DEY
  - Shifts/rotates: ASL, LSR, ROL, ROR (accumulator and memory)
  - Control flow: JMP (absolute and indirect, including the real
    6502's indirect-JMP page-wrap hardware bug), JSR, RTS, all 8
    branches, BRK, RTI
  - Flags: CLC, CLD, CLI, CLV, SEC, SED, SEI, NOP
  - Stack pointer correctly increments/decrements on push/pop
  - Cycle counts implemented and tested for every instruction above,
    including page-cross penalties on absolute,X/Y and (indirect),Y

Known limitations:
  - No BCD (decimal mode) arithmetic. The D flag is tracked but
    ADC/SBC always do binary math.
  - No illegal/undocumented opcodes (e.g. LAX, SAX).
  - Cycle counts are verified for the cases covered by the test
    suite; not every addressing-mode/page-cross combination has an
    explicit test yet (see TODO).

FILES
  cpu6502.h        - Mem and CPU structs; the emulator itself
  main.cpp         - demo program (#include "cpu6502.h")
  test_cpu6502.cpp - unit test suite (doctest), 44 test cases
  doctest.h        - vendored single-header test framework
  Makefile         - build/test/run targets

BUILD
  make
  (equivalent by hand: g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra main.cpp -o emu)

RUN
  make run
  ./emu
The included main() loads a small hardcoded program: JSR to 0x4242,
which runs LDA #0x10, CLC, ADC #0x05, STA $10, LDX $10, RTS. After
execution: A = 0x15, X = 0x15, mem[0x10] = 0x15.

TEST
  make test
  (equivalent by hand: g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -I. test_cpu6502.cpp -o test_cpu6502 && ./test_cpu6502)
44 test cases / 81 assertions covering every instruction family,
including flag behavior (signed overflow on ADC, borrow on SBC),
wraparound on zero-page,X and INX/DEX, branch taken/not-taken/
backward-loop cycle costs, the indirect-JMP page-wrap bug, and a
full BRK -> RTI round trip. Writing this suite caught two real bugs
(BRK and RTI were each 2 cycles short, which let leftover cycle
budget spill into executing the next instruction) that a simple
smoke-test main() did not surface.

DEBUGGING
Useful things to check when adding new opcodes:
  - Cycles is a signed int (s32). If an opcode's cycle count doesn't
    match what Execute() is told to run for, the while loop can run
    short (executing part of the next instruction on leftover
    budget) or long.
  - Watch PC and Cycles in gdb/pwndbg when a new opcode misbehaves:
        break CPU::Execute
        watch Cycles
        watch PC
  - Inspect raw memory directly:
        x/16xb mem.Data+0xFFFC
  - When a new opcode's behavior looks right but a test around it
    fails intermittently or with a nonsensical PC/SP, check its
    cycle count first -- a short count is the most common cause and
    was the source of both bugs found so far (BRK, RTI).

